find /home/oracle -inum 12345 --> the path to the file with the inode number 12345.

ls -li test.txt --> find test.txt inode along with its number.

So you can work in both directions:
===================================
Filename → inode
----------------
test.txt → 12345

inode → filename
----------------
12345 → /home/oracle/test.txt



ls -ldi /tmp/inodedirectory --> Display the inode number of a directory
"ls -ldi ." -->Display the inode number of the current directory
stat . --> Display detailed information about the current directory
find /tmp/inodedirectory -maxdepth 1 -printf '%i %p\n' --> Display inode number and path using find
